        "opening_words": "You're sitting at the kitchen table with\nyour partner doing that thing couples do\nwhere you stare at your bank account and\ntry to figure out where all the money\nwent. Between the two of you, you're\npulling in $130,000\na year. That's more than your parents\nmade combined, probably more than your\ngrandparents could have imagined. You\nshould be crushing it.\nBuilding wealth, taking vacations,\nfeeling secure. Instead, you're stressed\nabout whether you can afford new tires",
        "ending_words": "\nThe escape route isn't mysterious. Never\nlet your fixed costs require both\npaychecks. Drive the older car, live in\nthe smaller house, skip the prestigious\nschool district, and bank the\ndifference. Every dollar you don't lock\ninto fixed costs is a dollar that buys\nyou options, flexibility, resilience.\nThe two-income trap won't announce\nitself. You'll sign the mortgage, enroll\nthe kids, finance the cars, and one day\nyou'll be sitting at your kitchen table\nwondering why you feel broke despite\ndoing everything right. The only way out\nis seeing the trap before you're in it,\nand now you've seen it.",
